#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158391} :activity/id #uuid "c2c46d67-88f2-5cb5-ad0b-e155cb945332" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050450 :topic/id #uuid "49ef522d-8ba6-502a-8a64-c19ff9a6b681" :topic/math-academy-id 2818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158467 :tutorial/content "For a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \nwe know that if $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{black}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{black}\\lambda_2},$ respectively, then the general solution formula is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut what happens if $A$ has a single repeated eigenvalue, $\\lambda?$ It depends on the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **complete** eigenvalue if there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\n- We say that $\\lambda$ is a **defective** (or **incomplete**) eigenvalue if there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v$ corresponding to $\\lambda.$\n\nThis leads to two distinct cases:\n\n- *The Complete Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *complete* eigenvalue, then there are two linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to $\\lambda,$ and we can use the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda} t}\n$$\n\n- *The Defective Case.* If $\\lambda$ is a *defective* eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v.$ We cannot use the usual formula because there are not enough eigenvectors.\n\nInstead, we must find a *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf w,$ which is a vector that satisfies the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is then given by the formula: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, )\n$$\n\nWe'll explain how and why this solution comes about at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "76c4a128-aa18-5cb4-a460-0811386673d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4281 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fb2bbd4a-aeae-553c-ac02-210278766cbb" :step/math-academy-id 550835 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157073 :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351185}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"} :step/id #uuid "59fd701c-e0be-5da0-8006-b206fcd15119" :step/math-academy-id 550836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157074 :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "bf1cdfb1-e3d1-56f0-b6a6-775a91e83782" :step/math-academy-id 550839 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050098}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394092}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "4b9a873f-47e9-56dd-84a0-2bf953ccd698" :step/math-academy-id 550842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158468 :tutorial/content "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nThe reason why we must use this form is that if we were to guess [math]\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}[/math] as a second fundamental solution [math]\\textrm x_2(t),[/math] then we would get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) &= A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\\\[3pt]\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} \\left[ \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\right] &= A \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t}+ \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  t e^{\\lambda t} &= \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t} &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v} &= 0.\n\\end{align*} \n\nBut we can't have [math]\\mathbf v = \\mathbf 0,[/math] since [math]\\mathbf v[/math] is an eigenvector, and an eigenvector can't be the zero vector! So this guess will not work.\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{'}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." :tutorial/id #uuid "044881e6-feac-543e-b223-ea1a1c9da634" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4282 :tutorial/title "Justification for the Solution Formula in the Defective Case"} :step/id #uuid "37b8ee3e-0296-5c88-ad97-3614e478781f" :step/math-academy-id 550845}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157476}]}
